            {
              "id": "3a1889bf-9048-3cfd-a24c-2b8bba4ccde4",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the function `atof()` is used, which converts a string to a floating-point number. The `atof()` function does not perform any error checking, and if the input string is not a valid floating-point number, it can lead to undefined behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using C library functions that do not perform error checking. Instead, use safer alternatives that are available in C++. For example, instead of using `atof()`, you can use `std::stof()` or `std::stod()`, which throw an exception if the conversion fails.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atof()` function with `std::stof()` or `std::stod()`. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\n// ...\n\ntry {\n    alpha = std::stof(argv[argIdx++]);\n} catch (const std::invalid_argument& ia) {\n    // Handle the exception\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: For the `atof()` function.\n- `<string>`: For the `std::stof()` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
